/E (At the heart of electronic devices are materials carefully engineered for their specific properties. The relevance of ongoing research in this field extends beyond the confines of traditional electronics, influencing diverse sectors such as healthcare, renewable energy, and computing. The potential of semiconductor research is vast, offering opportunities for groundbreaking discoveries, technological revolutions, and solutions to pressing global challenges.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
226 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The potential inherent in semiconductor research lies in its ability to push the boundaries of what is technologically possible. Advancements in materials science, such as the exploration of novel materials like graphene and quantum dots, open avenues for developing faster, smaller, and more energy-efficient devices. Quantum computing, a field within semiconductor research, has the potential to revolutionize computation, solving complex problems that are currently beyond the reach of classical computers.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The dynamic nature of semiconductor research presents numerous opportunities for breakthrough discoveries. Researchers are continually exploring new materials, fabrication techniques, and device architectures. Current research is focused on developing new materials or enhancing existing ones to meet the ever-increasing demands of technology. Innovations range from the exploration of two-dimensional materials like graphene and transition metal dichalcogenides to the synthesis of complex organic compounds with unique electronic characteristics.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Like the rest of the world, researchers at our MEMS department are greatly enthused by this field and have been carrying out exciting research in our department labs. Given that electronic materials science is an extremely vast field, in this article, we try to explore the research done by one of the research groups in our department in detail. This article addresses some of the common questions that budding UG researchers have regarding this field with the help of one of our department professors working on electronic materials: Prof. Rajiv O. Dusane \(Head of Semiconductors, Thin Films and Plasma Processing Laboratory\), who shares his experience of doing research in semiconductors and thin films, and provides guidance for students to progress in this field.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
