242 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Strains in hetero-epitaxial systems alters its electronic and optoelectronic properties because of the difference in the lattice parameters of the film and the substrate. Thus, they can be employed in the exciting field of bandgap engineering. However, in certain applications, the existence of residual stresses can be detrimental, causing the films to delaminate or crack. Thus, a sound understanding on how these stresses develop becomes essential. Consequently, I started working on Mechanical Stresses in Thin Films. As described by Pramanik \(2011\), the residual stresses in thin films are a consequence of the coupling of thermal mismatch, lattice mismatch and misfit dislocations at the interface. At the beginning of the film growth, elastic energy, stored in the film due to lattice mismatch, is too small for nucleation of dislocations. As the film thickness is growing up dislocations begin to nucleate and it partially relaxes the strain accumulated in the film. Using COMSOL, I simulated the stresses in thin film systems as a consequence of the difference in the Coefficient of Thermal Contraction of the film and the substrate. )
/E (Strains in hetero-epitaxial systems alters its electronic and optoelectronic properties because of the difference in the lattice parameters of the film and the substrate. Thus, they can be employed in the exciting field of bandgap engineering. However, in certain applications, the existence of residual stresses can be detrimental, causing the films to delaminate or crack. Thus, a sound understanding on how these stresses develop becomes essential. Consequently, I started working on Mechanical Stresses in Thin Films. As described by Pramanik \(2011\), the residual stresses in thin films are a consequence of the coupling of thermal mismatch, lattice mismatch and misfit dislocations at the interface. At the beginning of the film growth, elastic energy, stored in the film due to lattice mismatch, is too small for nucleation of dislocations. As the film thickness is growing up dislocations begin to nucleate and it partially relaxes the strain accumulated in the film. Using COMSOL, I simulated the stresses in thin film systems as a consequence of the difference in the Coefficient of Thermal Contraction of the film and the substrate. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
243 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (This exercise aimed to perform FEM simulations and compare it to experimental findings. The thin film system is cooled from the deposition temperature to room temperature by convection, and they contract at different rates resulting in residual stresses. The von mises stress extracted from several positions in the film with respect to the interface revealed the independence of residual stresses with respect to distance from the interface. Currently, I am working on realizing a lab setup for fabricating films on flexible substrates, which is prominently used in flexible electronics and subjecting it to various forms of mechanical deformation, while studying the alterations in its properties. At the beginning of my research, everything felt intimidating. The idea of reading multiple papers, tackling concepts that I had never heard about felt challenging. Eventually, that initial friction went away and I could make progress. Attending meetings, and giving presentations on my research was an incredible learning experience which has enabled me to convey my ideas in an effective fashion.)
/E (This exercise aimed to perform FEM simulations and compare it to experimental findings. The thin film system is cooled from the deposition temperature to room temperature by convection, and they contract at different rates resulting in residual stresses. The von mises stress extracted from several positions in the film with respect to the interface revealed the independence of residual stresses with respect to distance from the interface. Currently, I am working on realizing a lab setup for fabricating films on flexible substrates, which is prominently used in flexible electronics and subjecting it to various forms of mechanical deformation, while studying the alterations in its properties. At the beginning of my research, everything felt intimidating. The idea of reading multiple papers, tackling concepts that I had never heard about felt challenging. Eventually, that initial friction went away and I could make progress. Attending meetings, and giving presentations on my research was an incredible learning experience which has enabled me to convey my ideas in an effective fashion.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
